 \begin{proof}
-Assume that $\hc$ is a Paley graph or a Peisert graph with $q > 9$ (the graphs on $5$ and $9$ vertices are homogeneous). 
-First assume $q \notin \{7^2,3^4,23^2\}$.
-It is well-known that in this case, we have $\Aut(\hc) \leq \AGammaL_1(q)$ and thus $\Aut(\hc)_{\vc} \leq \GammaL_1(q)$. 
-Note that $\GammaL_1(q) \cong \F_q^\times \rtimes C_r$ does not have a section isomorphic to $S_4$ as the only cyclic normal subgroup of $S_4$ is the trivial group.
-By Remark~\ref{rem:factorialboundstabilizer}, every clique or independent set in $\gc$ thus has size at most 3. 
-By Remark~\ref{rem:Ramsey}, we have $|\gc_i| \leq 17$ for $i = 1,2$, so $|\gc| \leq 35$ and thus $|\hc| \leq 70$.
-The remaining small graphs are all covered by our previous results. 
-For $q = 7^2$, we have $|\Aut(\hc)_{(v,w)}| = 2$ for every pair of distinct vertices $v,w \in V(\gc)$. 
-As $|\gc| \geq 24$, $\gc$ contains at least five vertices with the same neighbouring relation to $v$ and $w$, on which $\Aut(\hc)_{(v,w)}$ must thus act transitively. 
-This is a contradiction. 
-The same argument works for $q = 23^2$. For $q = 3^4$, the stabiliser of a pair of distinct vertices has order $4$. 
-We can then argue as before.
